    "Stem": "Grandview Enterprises controller Elaine Park has completed the annual internal control assessment and identified four unremediated deficiencies from the prior year's audit. The audit committee has authorized remediation funding of $180,000 for the current fiscal year, which Park must allocate across competing priorities. The four deficiencies are:\n\n1. Procure-to-pay three-way match: System bypasses the purchase order step for 22% of invoices under $5,000 when entered by senior buyers. No dollar loss has been documented, but the auditor estimates $8,000–$15,000 in annual duplicate payments based on peer benchmarks. Remediation estimate: $42,000.\n\n2. Vendor master file access: Four purchasing clerks can create and modify vendor records without secondary approval. Last year, a clerk added a fictitious vendor, submitted $7,400 in false invoices, and was terminated. Controls have not been strengthened since the incident. Remediation estimate: $85,000.\n\n3. Bank reconciliation timing: Reconciliations for the primary operating account are completed 45–60 days after month-end due to staff workload. Cash disbursements average $2.1 million monthly. The external auditor has flagged this as a material weakness for two consecutive years. Remediation estimate: $28,000.\n\n4. Journal entry review: 34% of non-standard journal entries are posted without supervisory review during the first three business days of each month. No fraudulent entries have been found, but the volume of unreviewed entries is rising (~15 entries/month last year, ~22 entries/month this year). Remediation estimate: $25,000.\n\nPark must present her prioritized remediation plan to the audit committee next week. Which deficiency should receive the highest priority, and why?",
